   1: """For every limb P46 scores, does the thing it scores REACH A READER?
   2: 
   3: THE GENERAL FORM OF CLASS 83, WHICH WAS FOUND THREE TIMES IN ONE NIGHT IN THREE DIFFERENT
   4: VOCABULARIES BEFORE ANYBODY ASKED THE GENERAL QUESTION:
   5: 
   6:     limb 3   applier wrote `title`/`agreement`     projector read `citation`/`how_it_differs`
   7:              -> 16 rows across 13 topics rendered a PMID and four blank cells
   8:     limb 4   applier wrote `model_output.verbatim` projector read `by_outcome.*.r_output`
   9:              -> the manuscript refused "no analysis output is stored on this object"
  10:     limb 4   applier wrote `model_output.verbatim` SCORER read `by_outcome.*.r_output`
  11:              -> p46_queue returned ABSENT and two topics were REPORTED AS 4 OF 4 AT 3 OF 4
  12: 
  13: `p46_queue.score()` reads the OBJECT. It answers "does this topic possess the limb". It has
  14: never answered "can a reader open a page and find it", and every P46 count reported so far
  15: has meant the first while being heard as the second. THAT IS THE PRECISE DEFINITION OF
  16: "objects built is not topics a reader can open", committed by the people who wrote it down.
  17: 
  18: HOW THIS DECIDES "RENDERS", AND WHY IT IS THE ONLY TEST THAT CANNOT BE FOOLED. It takes a
  19: PROBE STRING OUT OF THE STORED LIMB ITSELF -- never typed here -- and looks for it in the
  20: DELIVERED BYTES of the topic's page. Not in the object. Not in the projector's source. Not in
  21: a list of section names. If the sentence the object stores is not in the file a reader
  22: downloads, the limb does not reach a reader, whatever anything else says.
  23: 
  24:     probe = the longest run of HTML-safe characters in the stored limb, >= 40 chars
  25: 
  26: Quotes, ampersands and angle brackets are escaped on the way out, so a probe containing them
  27: would report a false absence. The probe is chosen to avoid them rather than the comparison
  28: being loosened -- a check that can only report "renders" is not a check.
  29: 
  30: SCORES ARE NEVER INVENTED HERE. `held` comes from importing p46_queue.score, so there is ONE
  31: definition of held in the repo and this file cannot drift from it.
  32: 
  33: FOUR STATES PER LIMB:
  34:     HELD+RENDERS   the object carries it and a reader can find it
  35:     HELD ONLY      the object carries it and the delivered page does not show it  <- class 83
  36:     NO PAGE        the topic has no page in PAGE_MAP, so nothing can be delivered
  37:     not held       p46_queue says REFUSED or ABSENT; delivery is not asked
  38: """
  39: import glob
  40: import importlib.util
  41: import io
  42: import json
  43: import os
  44: import re
  45: import sys
  46: 
  47: REPO = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
  48: sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
  49: from instrument_controls import require_controls          # noqa: E402
  50: 
  51: _spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(
  52:     "p46_queue", os.path.join(REPO, "scripts", "p46_queue.py"))
  53: p46 = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_spec)
  54: _spec.loader.exec_module(p46)
  55: 
  56: LIMBS = ["rob_per_result", "grade_per_pool", "comparison_denominator",
  57:          "model_output_verbatim"]
  58: 
  59: # THE MANUSCRIPT SECTION EACH LIMB IS PROJECTED INTO, by its rendered heading. Used ONLY for
  60: # refused limbs: the projector generates its own refusal wording rather than projecting the
  61: # object's, so a probe taken from the object cannot match, and the testable property is
  62: # narrower -- IS THE REFUSAL NAMED IN THIS LIMB'S OWN SECTION. Reported as REFUSED+NAMED, a
  63: # deliberately weaker claim than HELD+RENDERS.
  64: SECTION_HEADING = {
  65:     "rob_per_result": "Risk of bias in the included results",
  66:     "grade_per_pool": "Certainty of the evidence",
  67:     "comparison_denominator": "Comparison with published syntheses",
  68:     # THE HEADING THIS LIMB IS PROJECTED UNDER MOVED, AND THE PROBE HAS TO SURVIVE THAT.
  69:     #
  70:     # On 2026-08-21 the manuscript was reordered to F1000's canonical structure and this
  71:     # section became `Extended data: statistical output, quoted verbatim` -- the venue's own
  72:     # words for material supporting the key claims but not part of the main body. The old
  73:     # value was matched with `re.escape` against the raw page, so it stopped matching on TWO
  74:     # counts at once: a new prefix, and `Statistical` becoming lowercase `statistical`.
  75:     #
  76:     # A heading match that fails reports `the section heading is not on the page at all`,
  77:     # which the audit scores as REFUSAL LOST. So a purely cosmetic rename would have been
  78:     # reported as P46 limb 4 ceasing to reach readers on every topic that refuses it. That is
  79:     # the SEVENTEENTH lookup in this run to under-count by reading one spelling of a thing the
  80:     # corpus writes two ways, and the fifth to do it in a direction that reads as ABSENCE.
  81:     #
  82:     # Fixed at the resolver: the value is the STABLE TAIL of the heading and the search is
  83:     # case-insensitive, so a prefix the venue requires cannot blind the probe again.
  84:     "model_output_verbatim": "statistical output, quoted verbatim",
  85: }
  86: 
  87: 
  88: def refusal_named_in_section(page_text, heading):
  89:     """Does a `Refused:` block appear inside this section on the delivered page?
  90: 
  91:     The section runs from the LAST occurrence of its heading (the first is the in-page
  92:     navigation strip) to the next heading-like marker after it.
  93: 
  94:     MATCHED AS A HEADING ELEMENT, not as a bare substring, and case-insensitively.
  95: 
  96:     THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS FIX BROKE FOUR TOPICS AND THE MEASUREMENT CAUGHT IT. Making a
  97:     bare-substring search case-insensitive moved 4 topics from REFUSED+NAMED to REFUSED, NOT
  98:     NAMED, because this function is shared by all four limbs and `hits[-1]` assumes the LAST
  99:     mention is the heading. That assumption was being held up by capitalisation: `Risk of bias
 100:     in the included results` as a heading, `risk of bias in the included results` in prose
 101:     further down. Drop the case and the prose mention wins the `[-1]`, the span starts after
 102:     the real section, and a refusal that renders perfectly is reported missing.
 103: 
 104:     So the loosening is confined to where it belongs. Prose cannot appear inside an `<h2>`,
 105:     `<h3>` or `<h4>`, so anchoring on the element is immune to both failure modes at once: a
 106:     venue-required prefix cannot blind it, and a lowercase prose mention cannot steal it. It
 107:     also closes a defect the strict version already had -- a prose mention at the start of a
 108:     sentence is capitalised, and would have stolen the `[-1]` on any page that had one.
 109:     """
 110:     pat = r"(?is)<h[234][^>]*>[^<]*%s[^<]*</h[234]>" % re.escape(heading)
 111:     hits = [m.end() for m in re.finditer(pat, page_text)]
 112:     if not hits:
 113:         return False, "the section heading is not on the page at all"
 114:     start = hits[-1]
 115:     nxt = page_text.find("<h3", start)
 116:     span = page_text[start:nxt if nxt > start else start + 6000]
 117:     # TWO WAYS A REFUSAL REACHES A READER, AND ONLY ONE WAS RECOGNISED.
 118:     #
 119:     # The projector emits a `Refused:` block when it has NO field to project. But an object
 120:     # that refuses a limb DELIBERATELY -- storing state / why / what_would_close_it -- now has
 121:     # those projected as PROSE, which is a better refusal, not a missing one. Counting only
 122:     # the marker reported apixaban-vte-prophylaxis as REFUSAL LOST minutes after its refusal
 123:     # started rendering in full. A check that recognises one of two correct forms manufactures
 124:     # a defect out of an improvement.
 125:     named = ("Refused:" in span) or ("This limb is REFUSED" in span)
 126:     return named, ("a refusal is named in this section" if named
 127:                    else "the section renders with no refusal in it")
 128: 
 129: 
 130: def probe_is_distinctive(probe, page, other_pages):
 131:     """A probe found on OTHER topics' pages is boilerplate and proves nothing here.
 132: 
 133:     THE SECOND STATED LIMIT, CLOSED. A short or generic stored sentence can be shared across
 134:     objects -- house-rule wording, a ceiling statement, a standard refusal. Finding it in
 135:     this page would then say nothing about THIS limb reaching a reader. A probe that appears
 136:     on a page belonging to a different topic is reported WEAK rather than counted.
 137:     """
 138:     for p in other_pages:
 139:         if probe in p:
 140:             return False
 141:     return True
 142: 
 143: # Characters the renderer escapes on the way out. A probe containing one of these would be
 144: # looked for in a form the page never carries, and would report a false ABSENT.
 145: UNSAFE = re.compile(r"[<>&\"'â€˜â€™â€œâ€]")
 146: 
 147: 
 148: def probe_from(text, minimum=40):
 149:     """The longest HTML-safe run in `text`, or None if nothing long enough survives."""
 150:     if not isinstance(text, str):
 151:         return None
 152:     runs = [r.strip() for r in UNSAFE.split(text)]
 153:     runs = [r for r in runs if len(r) >= minimum]
 154:     return max(runs, key=len) if runs else None
