 210: 
 211: # WHY A LIST AND NOT THE LONGEST STRING -- THE THIRD PROBE-DESIGN CORRECTION IN THIS FILE.
 212: #
 213: # The first version returned the single longest HTML-safe run under the limb. That is not the
 214: # same as "a sentence the renderer emits", and for risk of bias it is systematically the wrong
 215: # one: the projector deliberately renders the OVERALL reason and the reasons for HIGH,
 216: # SOME_CONCERNS and LOW domains, and deliberately SKIPS NO_INFORMATION reasons -- which are
 217: # the longest strings in the block, because they carry the standing explanation of what this
 218: # review did not retrieve.
 219: #
 220: # So alirocumab, bempedoic-acid and iv-iron-hf were reported HELD ONLY on the strength of a
 221: # probe drawn from the one field guaranteed not to render. Twelve candidates now, and the
 222: # limb counts as reaching a reader if ANY of them is found. If none of twelve stored
 223: # sentences appears in the delivered bytes, the limb genuinely does not reach a reader.
 224: 
 225: 
 226: def page_map():
 227:     m = json.load(io.open(os.path.join(REPO, "ssot", "PAGE_MAP.json"), encoding="utf-8"))
 228:     rev = {}
 229:     for page, objpath in m.items():
 230:         rev.setdefault(os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(objpath)), []).append(page)
 231:     return rev
